## Version ↔ RFC ↔ issue (three layers)

| Layer | Owns | Slips how? |
|-------|------|------------|
| **Backlog** `0.7.6-[open].md` | Which items ship in which semver | Move item to next version file; close old version honestly |
| **RFC** `003-[partial]-….md` | Feature spec + full acceptance | **Same file** — status changes only when implementation state changes |
| **Issue** `018-[open]-….md` | Bug, debt, verifiable sub-work | **Same file** — `[fixed]` or `[canceled]` when done or abandoned |

**Version assignment lives in backlog**, not in RFC/issue filenames. Optional in RFC/issue body: `**Target version:** 0.7.7`.

### Active backlog — scope big work before coding

When the **active open/partial backlog** is in context (e.g. next `[open].md` under `docs/backlog/`) or the user is clearly shipping that version arc, and they ask for something **substantial** — **create or extend docs first**, then implement. Do not jump straight to code without a tracked spec.

**Substantial = needs an RFC or issue:**

| Kind | Create | Examples |
|------|--------|----------|
| **RFC** | New `docs/rfc/NNN-[draft\|planned\|open\|partial]-….md` | New feature subsystem, multi-component architectural change, new acceptance slice that does not fit an existing RFC |
| **Issue** | New `docs/issues/NNN-[draft\|open]-….md` | Concrete bug, engine debt, test gap, verifiable sub-work with its own fix |
| **Extend existing** | Add rows to **existing** RFC/issue tables | Fits current RFC-023 scope → add `R23-A*` / `B80-S*` rows; do **not** clone a new RFC number |

**Same turn — mandatory checklist when creating:**

1. **Pick next free number** — grep `docs/rfc/` and `docs/issues/` for highest NNN; use NNN+1
2. **Create the file** — full mandatory format ([Progress tracker](#progress-tracker-format-mandatory--all-layers), glance, legend, emoji task tables). New RFCs start `[draft]` or `[planned]`; new issues `[draft]` until work starts → `[open]`
3. **Wire into active backlog** — add row to `## RFCs` or `## Issues` (slice + status after ship); add ⬜ row(s) to `## Shipped` with IDs linking to the new spec
4. **Update indexes** — [RFC README](../../docs/rfc/README.md) and/or [issues README](../../docs/issues/README.md); [backlog README](../../docs/backlog/README.md) if version scope changed
5. **Link back** — RFC/issue `**Target version:**` → active backlog file; backlog glance **Blocks close** / deferred if out of scope for this ship

**If the ask is too big for the current version:** still create the RFC/issue, add to backlog `## Deferred → later X.Y.x` or create/next patch `[draft].md` — do not silently cram unrelated work into the open version without a backlog line.

**If the ask is small** (one file, obvious fix inside an existing RFC slice): extend that RFC’s task table + backlog Shipped row — no new RFC/issue file.

**Do not wait** for the user to say “create the RFC” — if the work is big and untracked, file the doc and backlog entry in the **same turn** as you start (or before) implementation.

---

1. Move old backlog → `done/X.Y.Z-[done].md` with **Shipped** and **Deferred → next version** sections
2. Create/update next backlog (e.g. `0.7.7-[draft].md`) with deferred items
3. RFC/issue stays `[partial]` / `[open]` / `[draft]` — do **not** `[fixed]` or `[canceled]` unless work is done or killed
4. `[canceled]` on backlog = version dropped; `[canceled]` on RFC/issue = will never build/fix

### Partial RFC shipped across versions — do not split

Ship incrementally from **one RFC per feature**. Use slices inside the spec, not new RFC numbers.

**Inside the RFC:**

- `## Components` + `## Acceptance (slice)` emoji task tables — flip row Status when code ships
- Optional `## Slices` prose mapping ID ranges to ship batches

**In backlog:**

- Shipped rows name slice + IDs: `RFC-023: ShellScaffold (R23-C02) ✅`
- Deferred references open row IDs: `R23-A10–A14 device smoke`

**RFC status:**

- Stays `[partial]` until **all** acceptance rows ✅ for that RFC → then `[fixed]`
- Do **not** rename to `[fixed]` because one slice shipped in a version
- Backlog `[done]` can honestly list partial RFC slices as shipped

### When to split vs nest

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Same feature, shipped in chunks across versions | **One RFC** — slices/phases inside |
| Different subsystem, independent ship/cancel | **Separate RFCs** (e.g. sync vs watch party) |
| Release theme / milestone index | **Meta RFC** (e.g. v1.2 bundle) — links to child RFCs; spec stays in each child |
| Concrete bug, test gap, debt with own fix | **Child issue** (parent links 019–022 pattern) |
| “Ship this slice in 0.7.7” | **Backlog Shipped row** linking to RFC/issue ID |

**Do not** invent IDs outside the prefixes in [Progress tracker format](#progress-tracker-format-mandatory--all-layers). Migration `P2-XX` IDs stay in migration docs only.

**Do not:** clone an RFC for the next version; put semver in RFC filenames; use `[canceled]` when you mean deferred to next version.

